```
$ jci-coverage complexity --help
Computes the cyclomatic complexity of the Rust functions a Cobertura
report covers and writes it into each method, where OtterWise reads it
for its Complexity and CRAP scores. `cargo llvm-cov` leaves every
complexity at 0. Reads the source files the report names, relative to
`--source-root`, and needs no other tool. Rust only: it refuses a
report that is not a `cargo llvm-cov` Cobertura report of `.rs` files.
Run it after `report` has written one, and before `upload`.

Usage: jci-coverage complexity [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --file <PATH>
          Cobertura report to annotate.
          
          The Cobertura file `report` writes.
          
          [default: coverage/cobertura.xml]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase logging verbosity

      --output <PATH>
          Write the annotated report here instead of over `--file`.
          
          The default replaces the report in place; a failed run never
          leaves a half-written file.

  -q, --quiet...
          Decrease logging verbosity

      --source-root <DIR>
          Directory the report's source file paths are relative to
          
          [default: .]

      --duplicates <DUPLICATES>
          What to do with the several entries `cargo llvm-cov` writes
          for one generic function

          Possible values:
          - merge: One entry per function: complexity is a property of
            the source, so it should not depend on how many
            instantiations the tests happen to make
          - keep:  Leave every instantiation as `cargo llvm-cov` wrote
            it. OtterWise sums every entry, so a generic function
            counts once per instantiation
          
          [default: merge]

      --rules <RULES>
          Which decision points count

          Possible values:
          - rca:    Matches `rust-code-analysis`: every match arm and
            every `?` counts
          - mccabe: Textbook McCabe: a `match` adds one less than its
            arms, and `?` is not a decision
          
          [default: rca]

      --unmatched <UNMATCHED>
          What to do with a method that has no matching function in
          the source

          Possible values:
          - keep: Leave its complexity at `0`
          - one:  Count it as `1`, the complexity of a function with
            no decisions
          - fail: Fail the run
          
          [default: keep]

      --no-rollup
          Leave the class, package and root complexity as they are.
          
          By default they are set to the sum of the methods below
          them, so other Cobertura consumers see consistent data.
          OtterWise ignores them.

      --exclude-tests
          Count `#[cfg(test)]` code and `#[test]` functions as
          complexity 0

      --warn-complexity <N>
          Warn about each function whose cyclomatic complexity is at
          least this.
          
          A per-line average can hide a few very risky functions among
          many simple ones. 0 turns the check off.
          
          [default: 10]

      --warn-crap <N>
          Warn about each function whose CRAP score is at least this.
          
          CRAP is complexity squared times the uncovered share cubed,
          plus complexity, from the covered share of the function's
          own lines. 0 turns the check off.
          
          [default: 30]

      --max-complexity <N>
          Fail if any function's cyclomatic complexity is at least
          this.
          
          The report is written first (not with `--dry-run`, which
          still fails). 0 turns the check off.
          
          [default: 0]

      --max-crap <N>
          Fail if any function's CRAP score is at least this.
          
          A function with no executable lines has no CRAP score and
          never fails this. 0 turns the check off.
          
          [default: 0]

      --baseline [<PATH>]
          Let the functions listed in this file stay over a maximum.
          
          A function in the baseline fails only if it has got worse
          than it was recorded in a score that is at or over its
          maximum (a CRAP that moves while under `--max-crap` does not
          fail a function accepted for its complexity). It is still
          warned about, and one whose scores moved from the recorded
          ones is reported (worse or better) without failing. Without
          a value it reads `.jci-coverage-baseline` in the directory
          this runs from, by convention the repository root. Make the
          file with `--write-baseline`; it is a binary file for this
          tool, not meant to be edited.

      --write-baseline [<PATH>]
          Record the functions now over a maximum in this file, and do
          not fail.
          
          Needs `--max-complexity` or `--max-crap`. Without a value it
          writes `.jci-coverage-baseline` in the directory this runs
          from, by convention the repository root. Nothing is written
          with `--dry-run`.

      --summary-file <PATH>
          Write a short markdown summary of the gate result to this
          file.
          
          The failing functions (or that the gate passes) in a form
          for a pull request comment: the headline, a compact table,
          and a pointer to the CI log. Written only when requested,
          when a maximum is set, after the report; nothing is written
          with `--dry-run` or `--write-baseline`. A failure to write
          it is a warning and does not change the result.

      --pr-comment
          Post the gate summary as a comment on the pull request.
          
          The same summary as `--summary-file`, posted to the pull
          request this build is for (from `CIRCLE_PULL_REQUEST`),
          using the token in `GITHUB_TOKEN`. An earlier comment from
          this tool is edited in place, otherwise one is created,
          whether the gate passes or fails, so the pull request shows
          that the gate ran. Nothing is posted, and a reason is
          logged, when there is no token or no pull request, or the
          pull request is from a fork. A failure to post is a warning
          and does not change the result.

      --gate-tests
          Judge test code against the warning and maximum thresholds
          too

      --check-only
          Only run the maximum and baseline checks, and leave the
          report as it is.
          
          For a gate step after `upload`, so a failure comes after the
          upload and not before it. The report is not rewritten and
          the summary and warnings, which the annotating step has
          already given, are not repeated. With no maximum set there
          is nothing to check and it succeeds.

      --dry-run
          Work out and print the summary without writing anything

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
